WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.480
Film Courage: Looking back on it now, what
where the drawback and benefits to making

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.260
so many short films and do you think it was
worth it?

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:13.270
I know you wanted to make a feature film much
sooner.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:18.109
Peter Dukes, Writer/Director: For me personally
it’s worked out just fine.

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:20.640
I’ve taken an unusual path.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.910
I’ve taken a very patient path.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:27.880
I wanted to prepare myself as much as possible
about my short films as I’d mentioned before

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.960
and I didn’t.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.150
I didn’t want to get into the director’s
chair for feature and blow it.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:36.860
It’s hard enough to get that first chance.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.920
I didn’t want to get there and blow it and
never get another chance.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:46.930
For me it worked out nicely but it took 15
odd years (whatever it was…10 years)?

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:49.410
Whereas other people go I’m going to go
for it from the beginning.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:56.940
And again some of them make it early and since
they are younger than me…agencies love hot

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.480
up-and-coming talent.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.290
So if you are a young mid-twenties and you
make something really cool you’re going

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.270
to get more attention then if I make something
cool.

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:08.200
It’s just kind of the way the business (at
least the industry out here) is kind of set

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.200
up.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.200
I can’t say the way I did it is right or
wrong or what not.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.939
I’ve always been a patient guy.

00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:21.220
I’ve always taken my own road to things
so I think it’s been beneficial to me.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:28.220
Obviously the drawbacks are I’m 40 and only
have (what will soon be) two features under

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:33.600
my belt and if I’d taken a slightly different
path maybe I’d have had 10 at this point?

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.909
But that’s a drawback I’m willing to live
with.

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:42.039
I think I did it the only way that it was
ever going to work for me.

00:01:42.039 --> 00:01:46.729
It’s an interesting question because if
you ask 10 other filmmakers what they think

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:53.780
of my strategy they might laugh their heads
of from what I know (I don’t know?)

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.670
But it’s a weird business.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:56.670
Everyone is on their own.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:57.670
You are totally on your own.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.979
You can read as many books on the subject
as you want but the reality is no one is going

00:02:00.979 --> 00:02:01.979
to give you anything.

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:06.580
You’ve got to go out there and earn it through
talent and hard work, perseverance, all of

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.239
those kind of things.

00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:13.670
Every filmmaker (myself included), every single
filmmaker I’ve ever met literally has their

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:17.750
own fascinating story as to how they got it
done, how they got noticed.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:21.610
And some of them might be more successful
with others in terms of how far they’ve

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.810
risen or what level of movies they’re doing
now.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:31.299
But all of them just literally found their
way through the labyrinthian maze that is

00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:35.069
Hollywood and made it wherever they are these
days.

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:39.371
Film Courage: Can you recommend any resources
for young filmmakers starting out?

00:02:39.371 --> 00:02:41.600
I know you said previously everyone has their
own path.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:48.910
Are there resources that have been valuable
for you in terms of DIY, not only learning

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:51.069
about film theory or whatever?

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:57.450
Peter: Listen, I’m sure there are a million
books on the subject and I read many in college

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:03.540
that were perfectly fine, literally in terms
of people from a nuts-and-bolts, what do I

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.540
do?

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.700
How do I start directing at all or whatever?

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.459
I would recommend just getting on set, get
on as a PA (production assistant), get on

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:17.599
whenever you can, figure out how a [film]
set works.

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:19.030
You don’t have to become an expert at it.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:20.510
You don’t have to spend five years on set.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:26.040
But get on the set and figure out the world
you are going to be living in and leading

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.370
at some point as certainly as a director.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:36.540
Know that world and that is the very seedling
to you starting to put together your own world.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:42.510
I would recommend working at places that will
teach you what you need to know.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:45.599
If you’re not on set go work at a camera
rental house.

00:03:45.599 --> 00:03:47.549
Learn the gear.

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:52.020
If you want to get into writing and producing,
work at an agency, work at a management company,

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.569
work at a production company.

00:03:54.569 --> 00:03:58.200
I would learn who is in your business, who
do you want to know?

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.920
There are a lot of assistants out there.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.860
Figure out who works for who, network.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.760
In terms of gear, become an expert at it.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.930
You don’t have to become someone who is
paid $1,000 dollars a day to run the gear

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.170
but know it from a general standpoint.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:15.000
Know what you need, what you don’t need.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:21.680
If you’re writing, I would say one of the
best steps you could do is read great screenplays.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.990
They are all online these days anyhow.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.561
Read THE GODFATHER, read whatever you can
read.

00:04:26.561 --> 00:04:28.710
Read new scripts, old scripts.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:31.169
Read, read, read, and then read some more.

00:04:31.169 --> 00:04:33.180
Figure out what made them great.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.830
Then start writing yourself and write and
write and write.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:41.169
It’s probably going to be pretty awful at
first, it will probably get better until you

00:04:41.169 --> 00:04:42.889
start to find your own voice.

00:04:42.889 --> 00:04:48.180
That’s what you do whether you are learning
the gear and pulling it together to start

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.360
tackling producing and directing your own
short film.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.419
Whether you’re writing your own scripts,
don’t be intimidated by it, don’t be scared

00:04:55.419 --> 00:04:56.419
by it.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:05:00.639
Just do it and then you do it again and then
you do it again and again and keep honing

00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:05.280
it until you learn what you need to and I
think both will learn along the way more so

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.039
than most books can tell you, learn through
experience.

00:05:08.039 --> 00:05:11.650
Film Courage: You say that horror sparks conversation?

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:17.000
Peter: I think a great story sparks conversation.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:22.220
I think horror can spark certain visceral
reactions.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:30.550
I think a great film…my dream as a filmmaker
is to have one of my scripts or one of my

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:36.130
films cause an audience to leave the theater
and go talk about it and debate about it and

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:37.130
think about it.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:41.811
I don’t necessarily want to make the type
of movie that they eat their popcorn, they

00:05:41.811 --> 00:05:46.060
walk away and by the time they get to the
car they don’t remember what they saw (some

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:47.470
fun movie or whatever).

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:53.949
I think any great story (horror included)
is something that will make people talk and

00:05:53.949 --> 00:05:55.199
I mentioned privilege before.

00:05:55.199 --> 00:06:01.490
I do think it’s a privilege to be in a position
where I can build stories and characters and

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:08.490
tell stories that I think aim to make you
see the world and your place in it from maybe

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:09.490
a different perspective.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:15.360
I think that’s a very powerful thing that
we do and have the ability to do and I aspire

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.690
to do an even better than I do so now.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:21.629
But yes in short I think any great film should
spark discussion.

00:06:21.629 --> 00:06:22.880
I think that’s the goal.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.950
I think you should do something that inspires
you or traumatizes you or scares you.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:31.300
Whatever reaction is the point of your story
I think that should always be the point (to

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:36.500
get a reaction) otherwise your film is just
putting people to sleep.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.210
So that’s what I aim for.

